    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Bacteria interact with human-produced lipid sterols and lipids in a variety of ways that broadly impact human health. As such, identifying proteins involved in these interactions may inform new targets for therapeutic development. Historically, it has been difficult to identify proteins within bacterial genomes which may interact with lipid molecules. Large differences in structures and sequences of lipid-interacting proteins between eukaryotes and prokaryotes further complicates this task, so while eukaryotic interactions with cholesterol have been well studied, it is difficult to translate this prior work into bacteria. Recently, Enhygromyxa salina, a marine bacterium has been identified as a novel cholesterol producing bacterium. While E. salina is not biologically relevant to humans, E. salina may serve as a useful model system for understanding mechanisms by which biologically relevant bacteria interact with lipids. The overarching objective of this research is to utilize a novel tool to predict lipid binding proteins followed by biochemical and biophysical analyses to characterize lipid-interacting proteins in E. salina. In this study a YceI-like and DUF2071 domain containing protein were identified and characterized using these approaches. The results of this study begin to establish a workflow for identifying lipid interactomes within large bacteria genomes and provide initial results on the types of lipid molecules that may interact with these uncharacterized proteins.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
